hi everyone  this is professor m science and today i  want to talk about  constants of motion in another one of  our videos on rigorous quantum mechanics  as the name says a constant of motion is  a quantity that doesn't change  as the system evolves in time if you've  studied classical mechanics then most  probably you'll have encountered  constants of motion for example  the angular momentum of a classical  particle moving in a central potential  is conserved central forces include  gravity and electrostatic interaction  and one of the consequences of angular  momentum conservation  is that the trajectory of a particle  stays in a plane today we'll explore  what the constant of motion  is in quantum mechanics let's go  you'll probably be familiar with  constant of motion in classical  mechanics  in the classical context constants of  motion are quantities that are conserved  throughout the motion  in quantum mechanics however the  definition is more subtle  we say that an observable a is a  constant of motion  if it obeys two conditions the first  is that it does not explicitly depend on  time so that its time derivative  vanishes and the second  is that it commutes with the hiltonian  and this is it any observable a that  obeys these two conditions is a constant  of motion  in quantum mechanics the first condition  makes perfect sense  the observable that we're considering  should not explicitly depend on time if  it is to be a constant of motion  the second condition may appear less  obvious but we should remember that in  quantum mechanics time evolution is  given by the schrodinger equation  which tells us that the rate of change  of quantum states is governed by the  hamiltonian  from that point of view it sort of makes  sense that the hamiltonian should  feature somewhere in this definition  and in the rest of the video we'll see  how this second condition leads to a  series of properties that together  mean that an observable can indeed be  called a constant of motion  the first property of an observable that  is a constant of motion  is that its expectation value with  respect to any state  is time independent  to see this we need to remember the  expression for the time dependent of the  expectation value  of the general operator q it is given by  two terms  one proportional to the expectation  value of the commutator of q with the  hamiltonian of the system  and the other the expectation value of  the time derivative of q  we'd write this expression in the video  on iron fest theorem so check it out if  you need a refresher  if we now use this expression for an  observable a that is a constant of  motion  we get that the time derivative of the  expectation value of a  is equal to a term proportional to the  expectation value of the commutator of a  with the hamiltonian  plus the expectation value of the time  derivative of the observable  a but by definition of a constant of  motion  a commutes with a hamiltonian so this  term vanishes  and also by definition of a constant of  motion a is time  independent so this derivative also  vanishes  this means that the time derivative of  the expectation value of a constant of  motion  is overall zero  the next important property of a  constant of motion is that we can always  find the common set of eigenstates with  the hamiltonian  this property arises directly from the  fact that the constant of motion  commutes with the hamiltonian  we know from the video on compatible  observables that we can always find the  common set of eigenstates for two  observables that commute  this means that we can write the  eigenvalue equation for the hamiltonian  like this  and we can also write the eigenvalue  equation for the observable a like this  in these equations we label the energy  eigenvalues of the hamiltonian by  en and the eigenvalues associated with a  by  ap the eigenstates here and here  are the same for the hamiltonian and for  a because they commute we label this  common eigenstate with the quantum  number n  associated with the energy eigenvalue  and the quantum number p  associated with the a eigenvalue  from the video on the drawinger equation  we know that the eigenstate of the  hamiltonian  are called stationary states this is  because if the state psi of our system  at time t  0 is equal to an eigenstate of the  hamiltonian  then the state of the system at a later  time t is equal to this phase factor  times the same eigen state as a global  phase factor such as this one doesn't  change the predictions of quantum  mechanics  then the eigenstate of the hamiltonian  don't really change in time  and this is the reason why we call them  stationary states  the extra feature we have now is that  this initial state  is also an eigenstate of the operator a  with eigenvalue ap  labeled by this p here looking at the  state at a later time here  we see that it's still given by the c  eigenstate of a  and the usual phase factor that doesn't  change the physics  this means that if the system is in a  stationary state then the state of the  system is  also an eigenstate of a at all times  in turn this means that if we were to  measure a we would always get the same  eigenvalue  ap and for this reason the eigenvalues  of a constant of motion  the ap here are called good quantum  numbers  the final property associated with  constants of motion is that the  probability of measuring a particular  outcome is time  independent let's first write the  eigenvalue equation for the hamiltonian  we're going to assume we have a  conservative system in which the  hamiltonian is time  independent we also consider again the  eigenvalue equation for the constant of  motion  a let's now consider an arbitrary state  psi  at time t0 to study the time evolution  of this state we derived a recipe in the  video in the joiner equation for  conservative systems  the first step of the recipe is to  expand the initial state in the basis  spanned by the eigenstates of the  hamiltonian  as usual the expansion coefficients are  given by this bracket  we can now immediately construct the  state at a different time t  as given by this new expansion  where the expansion coefficients are  related to the original ones by a simple  phase factor  from these expressions we can now  calculate the probability of getting a  particular outcome when we measure a  at these different times the probability  of measuring  ap at time t 0 is equal to the absolute  value squared of the corresponding  expansion coefficient  i'm assuming that the eigenvalue is  non-degenerate but it would be trivial  to extend this discussion to the  degenerate case  the probability of measuring the same  eigenvalue at later time t  is now equal to this  using this expression up here for c and  p at time  t we can rewrite it like this  this phase factor has magnitude 1 so we  end up with this  comparing these two expressions we see  that the probability of getting  ap for a measurement at t 0 is the same  as the probability of getting ap  at a different time t overall this means  that when we measure a physical property  that is a constant of motion  then the probability of getting a  particular eigenvalue is the same  at any time  and that's it but let's recap before we  finish a constant of motion is a  physical quantity whose associated  observable obeys  two conditions the first is that it's  time  independent and the second is that it  commutes with the hamiltonian  from this definition we've then derived  the number of properties  associated with constants of motion we  found that their expectation value is  time  independent we've also found that the  eigenstates of a constant of motion  are time independent and we've also  found that the probability of a  measurement outcome of a physical  property that is a constant of motion  is time independent with these  properties you can now understand why we  call observables that obey these two  relations  constants of motion constants of motion  in quantum mechanics  are very useful because of the time  independence  of expectation values of eigenstates and  of measurement probability outcomes  as a concrete example angular momentum  is a constant of motion  in central potentials which is really  helpful when we try to then understand  the behavior of systems like the  hydrogen atom i hope you liked the video  and please subscribe

